1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,611
Good question to ask before spending an afternoon on a cut.

2
00:00:03,611 --> 00:00:08,253
Every placement so far in this section has been about earning attention from someone

3
00:00:08,253 --> 00:00:09,745
who wasn't looking for you.

4
00:00:09,745 --> 00:00:11,680
YouTube is frequently the opposite.

5
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:16,212
Someone searched a question, or clicked a video about a problem they already have,

6
00:00:16,212 --> 00:00:20,412
and your ad shows up inside that intent, not against a wall of indifference.

7
00:00:20,412 --> 00:00:22,125
That changes the job of a hook.

8
00:00:22,125 --> 00:00:25,607
On TikTok, the hook has to stop a scroll that has no direction.

9
00:00:25,607 --> 00:00:29,918
On YouTube's in-stream placements, the hook often has to survive a skip button

10
00:00:29,918 --> 00:00:34,450
instead, five seconds where a viewer who's already mid-task decides whether you're

11
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:36,937
relevant to what they actually came here for.

12
00:00:36,937 --> 00:00:39,203
Shorts breaks from that pattern entirely.

13
00:00:39,203 --> 00:00:43,846
It's YouTube's own vertical, scrolling, sound-off-by-default feed, closer in grammar

14
00:00:43,846 --> 00:00:47,991
to Reels and TikTok's For You page than to anything else on YouTube itself.

15
00:00:47,991 --> 00:00:52,136
Hold both of those facts at once, because they pull in opposite directions.

16
00:00:52,136 --> 00:00:55,507
In-stream rewards relevance to an intent someone already has.

17
00:00:55,507 --> 00:00:59,928
Shorts rewards exactly the native, unframed grammar Section 5.2 just taught you.

18
00:00:59,928 --> 00:01:04,571
One platform, two audiences behaving nothing alike, and neither one behaves like the

19
00:01:04,571 --> 00:01:06,450
platform's own name would suggest.

20
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,274
Two placements, two different disciplines, on one platform.

21
00:01:10,274 --> 00:01:13,436
Skippable in-stream ads live or die on that early window.

22
00:01:13,436 --> 00:01:17,542
Whatever convinces someone not to skip has to happen inside it, not after.

23
00:01:17,542 --> 00:01:22,203
Shorts plays by TikTok's rules almost exactly: vertical frame, sound off by default,

24
00:01:22,203 --> 00:01:26,697
a viewer who'll swipe past anything that reads as an ad before it earns the swipe

25
00:01:26,697 --> 00:01:27,640
past that reflex.

26
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:32,134
Budget production time accordingly: in-stream earns a dedicated cut only when you

27
00:01:32,134 --> 00:01:36,462
believe the intent argument, someone here already wants roughly what you sell.

28
00:01:36,462 --> 00:01:39,347
Shorts is close to free once your TikTok cut exists.

29
00:01:39,347 --> 00:01:43,287
Treating YouTube as one platform with one playbook is the mistake here.

30
00:01:43,287 --> 00:01:47,670
It's really two placements borrowed from two different logics, wearing the same

31
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:48,280
brand name.

32
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:51,998
Why does the skip button matter more than it sounds like it should?

33
00:01:51,998 --> 00:01:56,658
Because it hands the viewer an explicit exit that Meta and TikTok never offer in the

34
00:01:56,658 --> 00:01:57,158
same way.

35
00:01:57,158 --> 00:02:01,819
Scrolling past an ad on those platforms takes the same one-second flick as scrolling

36
00:02:01,819 --> 00:02:05,647
past anything else, an ambient decision nobody registers as a choice.

37
00:02:05,647 --> 00:02:09,864
A skip button is a deliberate, visible action: tap this, and the ad is gone.

38
00:02:09,864 --> 00:02:14,413
That's a higher bar to clear, and it's exactly why the first five seconds carry so

39
00:02:14,413 --> 00:02:16,577
much weight on this specific placement.

40
00:02:17,127 --> 00:02:19,956
Notice what doesn't change here as much as what does.

41
00:02:19,956 --> 00:02:23,478
The hook itself stays the same words, the same "hour eleven" line.

42
00:02:23,478 --> 00:02:27,694
What changes is what happens right after it: on in-stream, everything past five

43
00:02:27,694 --> 00:02:31,856
seconds is conditional on that hook actually working, because a skip button is

44
00:02:31,856 --> 00:02:35,058
sitting right there the whole time daring a viewer to leave.

45
00:02:35,058 --> 00:02:38,047
Shorts, by contrast, barely needs a separate cut at all.

46
00:02:38,047 --> 00:02:42,262
The same discipline that made Harlan's ad work on TikTok, sound-off, native, no

47
00:02:42,262 --> 00:02:46,638
framing that announces itself, transfers almost directly, because Shorts was built

48
00:02:46,638 --> 00:02:48,880
to compete with exactly that kind of feed.

49
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:50,961
That's a real efficiency worth banking.

50
00:02:50,961 --> 00:02:55,230
Of everything in your angle map, the version you cut for TikTok is very close to

51
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:59,713
done for Shorts too, same export, same captions, maybe a different on-screen handle.

52
00:02:59,713 --> 00:03:04,036
The in-stream version is the one that actually costs you a separate editing pass,

53
00:03:04,036 --> 00:03:08,198
because it's answering a different question than every other placement in this

54
00:03:08,198 --> 00:03:08,625
section.

55
00:03:08,625 --> 00:03:12,307
Think about what that separate pass actually needs to do differently.

56
00:03:12,307 --> 00:03:15,189
It's not a shorter or longer version of the same hook.

57
00:03:15,189 --> 00:03:19,458
It's a version that answers "why should I keep watching this specific ad" rather

58
00:03:19,458 --> 00:03:23,887
than "why should I stop scrolling", a subtly different question that rewards a more

59
00:03:23,887 --> 00:03:25,915
direct, less atmospheric opening line.

60
00:03:26,465 --> 00:03:30,173
One honest question before you build a separate cut for this platform.

61
00:03:30,173 --> 00:03:33,245
Harlan's socks aren't something people search for by name.

62
00:03:33,245 --> 00:03:37,536
Nobody's typing "graduated compression socks for twelve-hour shifts" into YouTube

63
00:03:37,536 --> 00:03:39,337
before they've heard of the brand.

64
00:03:39,337 --> 00:03:43,680
That doesn't rule YouTube out, Shorts still works exactly like TikTok does, but it

65
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:48,077
does mean the in-stream, intent-driven placement is a weaker fit here than it would

66
00:03:48,077 --> 00:03:52,102
be for a product people already know they want and are actively researching.

67
00:03:52,102 --> 00:03:56,393
Ask that question honestly about whatever you're selling before you spend a whole

68
00:03:56,393 --> 00:04:00,736
extra cut on a placement built around intent your audience might not actually have

69
00:04:00,736 --> 00:04:00,948
yet.

70
00:04:00,948 --> 00:04:05,291
There's a useful test here, borrowed straight from Section 2.2's awareness ladder:

71
00:04:05,291 --> 00:04:09,423
does your product sit closer to solution-aware or product-aware, where someone

72
00:04:09,423 --> 00:04:13,819
already knows roughly what they're looking for, or closer to problem-unaware, where

73
00:04:13,819 --> 00:04:17,898
nobody's searching for the answer yet because they haven't named the problem?

74
00:04:17,898 --> 00:04:20,758
The first case is where in-stream earns its extra cut.

75
00:04:20,758 --> 00:04:25,102
The second is where that budget is usually better spent on one more Shorts variant

76
00:04:25,102 --> 00:04:25,525
instead.

77
00:04:26,075 --> 00:04:31,117
Next lecture stops treating each platform separately and builds the one matrix that

78
00:04:31,117 --> 00:04:35,915
maps your whole creative set, Meta's ratios, TikTok's native cut, YouTube's two

79
00:04:35,915 --> 00:04:39,073
different jobs, to a single, repeatable export plan.

80
00:04:39,073 --> 00:04:42,110
Before that, take stock of what you actually have.

81
00:04:42,110 --> 00:04:46,727
If you followed Section 4.6's workshop, you've got three finished video ads,

82
00:04:46,727 --> 00:04:47,516
artifact B04.

83
00:04:47,516 --> 00:04:52,436
Sort them, right now, against the awareness-level column on your B02 hook matrix.

84
00:04:52,436 --> 00:04:57,538
That sort is what tells you, going into the next lecture, which of the three is even

85
00:04:57,538 --> 00:05:00,757
a candidate for YouTube's in-stream placement at all.
